    "product_id": "df6009a7-68a1-4899-97f3-b2645587be61",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 050807\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n105 AM MST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Major waterways such as the Salt and Gila rivers will continue to \nexperience high volumes flows through at least the the weekend due \nto dam releases.\n\n- With the exception of a slight chances of mountain showers on \nFriday, dry weather will be common through at least the middle of \nnext week.\n\n- Temperatures a few degrees below normal will warm very close to \nseasonal readings by the weekend.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Saturday/...\nA compact cyclonic circulation was situated over central Colorado \nearly this morning with an omega block pattern over the Conus \nbreaking down as southern stream jet energy forces a more \nprogressive flow pattern. Weak, elongated troughs will moves across \nthe Conus the next several days before another blocking type pattern \ndevelops over the weekend. Part of this evolution will include \nshortwave energy becoming disassociated with the progressive flow \nover the SW Conus dragging a weak cold core into northern Mexico and \nforming a partial cutoff while ridging amplifies over the Great \nBasin through the weekend. The passage of shortwave energy and \nincreased synoptic ascent Friday may be sufficient to squeeze out a \nfew showers over mountains of central and eastern Arizona, though \nmoisture availability will be extremely limited resulting in only \nvery low chances for light showers. Otherwise, the sharp anti-\ncyclonic flow behind the trough passage late Friday into Saturday \nwill promote strong surface pressure rises over the Great Basin \nyielding gusty north winds across the western CWA. H5 heights in a \n575-579dm range prior to trough passage will support temperatures \naround 4F below normal, followed by midlevel height rises and \nwarming of the lower troposphere over the weekend with reading \nrebounding close to daily normals. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Sunday through Wednesday/... \nSome variation of a Rex or omega block will define the southern \nstream flow pattern early next week as broad ridging dominates the \nnorthern Conus and northern stream jet energy retreats into southern \nCanada. While there are certainly differences among ensemble members \nregarding the exact placement and intensity of quasi-cutoff features \nin the southern stream, this synoptic pattern will promote surface \npressure rises over the intermountain West and dry N/NE boundary \nlayer flow. As a result, periods of breezy east winds are likely at \ntimes over the eastern CWA (very common occurrence this time of \nyear), though speeds should not be particularly strong or unusual. \nAt some point early next week, most ensemble members suggest the \naforementioned cutoff feature opening and residual energy moving \nthrough the region, though it remains to be seen whether an \nintroduction of mid/upper level moisture can overcome the dry \nboundary layer airmass. The greatest odds and large preponderance of \nensemble membership indicate dry weather persisting through the \nmiddle of the week. Forecast confidence is very good that H5 heights \nwill remain nearly constant in a 580-584dm range resulting in a near \npersistence temperature forecast with readings very near \nclimatology. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0520Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo aviation weather concerns are expected through the forecast \nperiod under mostly clear skies until FEW-SCT high clouds moving in \ntomorrow afternoon. Winds will remain light (generally 7 kts or \nless) and tend to follow typical diurnal patterns. Confidence in a \nwesterly shift tomorrow afternoon is low. Winds will likely be light \nand variable, but may tend to exhibit a W/NW component. Winds will \nthen redevelop an easterly component for the overnight hours. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation weather concerns are expected through the forecast \nperiod under mostly clear skies until FEW-SCT high clouds arrive \ntomorrow morning. Winds will remain light, generally 7 kts or less. \nWind directions at KBLH and KIPL will generally remain N-NW. \nExtended periods of calm and variable conditions can also be \nexpected.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAn isolated shower may affect mountains of eastern districts Friday, \nalbeit with little chance of wetting rainfall while the remainder of \nthe region remains dry through at least the middle of next week.\nMinimum afternoon humidity levels will largely fall into a 20-30% \nrange in western districts and 30-50% in eastern districts. This \nwill follow fair to good overnight recovery in western districts and \ngood to excellent levels in eastern districts. 20-30 mph northerly \ngusts will be common in western districts Friday night through \nSaturday, while a period of enhances easterly winds will develop \nover eastern districts early next week. Outside of these time \nframes, winds will be very light with little to no gustiness. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...18 \nAVIATION...Berislavich\nFIRE WEATHER...18\n",
